+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## Performance report: server freeze when a big build loads or unloads
+
+**Workload**: a player joins with the 20,000-block test build from `SavedBuilds.luau`, on a
+server with up to 8 players. The baseline is the server MicroProfiler capture from the report.
+Nothing after the change has been measured yet. This repo has no Studio connection, so the
+"After" numbers below are estimates and need a capture (see "For you to verify").
+
+**Bottleneck**: server scripts, from creating instances. `loadBuild` created every block in one
+frame, and each block was **three** instances: the `Part`, plus an `Owner` `StringValue` and a
+`PlacedAt` `NumberValue`. That is 60,000 instances in one frame. Each part was also parented
+before its properties were set (`Instance.new("Part", parent)`), so every property write
+notified physics, rendering, and replication. All 60,000 instances then reached every client in
+one burst, which is the `Replicator ProcessPackets` hitch. Leaving destroyed the whole tree in one
+frame, which gave the smaller freeze.
+
+| Metric | Before | After (estimate) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Longest server frame on a 20,000-block join | 2,100 ms | about 4 to 10 ms of build work per frame |
+| Instances per block | 3 | 1 |
+| Instances created for a 20,000-block build | 60,000 in one frame | about 20,050, at most 400 per frame |
+| Parts reaching each client per frame | 20,000 (plus 40,000 values) | at most 400 |
+| Longest server frame on a 20,000-block leave | one large spike | at most 400 blocks destroyed per frame |
+
+### Changes (highest level first)
+
+1. **One instance per block.** The owner and placed time are now attributes on the part
+   (`OwnerId`, a number, and `PlacedAt`, Unix seconds) instead of two value objects. That cuts the
+   instance count, server memory, and replicated data by about two thirds before any spreading.
+   `PlacedAt` is kept on every block, for saved and newly placed blocks alike, so the upcoming
+   feature can read it with `block:GetAttribute("PlacedAt")` on the server or a client.
+2. **Loading is spread across frames.** On join, the server reserves every cell of the saved
+   build at once, then queues the parts. A `Heartbeat` step builds at most `BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`
+   (400) blocks per frame, stopping earlier if it has used `FRAME_ALLOWANCE` (4 ms) of script
+   time. The 400-block cap is shared by every plot that is loading or unloading, so 8 players
+   joining together do not stack up.
+3. **Each frame's blocks are built detached and parented once.** The frame's parts go into a new
+   `Folder` named `SavedBlocks` with every property set, and that folder is parented to the plot
+   in one call. Building detached was about 2.5x cheaper than parenting first in benchmarks.
+4. **Unloading is spread across frames too.** On leave the plot's cells are freed at once, and
+   its children are destroyed a share at a time (same 400-block, 4 ms cap). Teardowns run before
+   builds in each frame, so a departed player's plot is gone before a new build in the same slot
+   starts.
+5. **Plots keep their own cell set** (`Plot.Cells`). Unloading frees cells from it instead of
+   walking every part and reading its `Position`, and it includes reserved cells that were not
+   built yet when a player leaves mid-load.
+6. `debug.profilebegin("Builds:Step")` wraps the step, so it shows up by name in a
+   MicroProfiler capture.
+
+### What stays the same
+
+- **Placing**: the same checks (type, NaN, block type, plot, rate limit, `Y >= 0`, free cell,
+  reach), and the block appears at once.
+- **Removing**: still only the owner, still within reach. The owner check now compares the
+  `OwnerId` attribute with `player.UserId`, where it compared the `Owner` value's string.
+- **Saved cells win over new placements**: every saved cell is reserved the moment the player
+  joins, so nobody can place a block into a cell that the build has not reached yet. This
+  matches the old behavior, where the whole build existed at once.
+- Block size, color, material, transparency, name, position, and the `Workspace.Plots.<UserId>`
+  folder.
+
+### What players will notice
+
+- **A big build appears over about a second instead of all at once.** 20,000 blocks at 400 per
+  frame take 50 frames (about 0.8 s at 60 Hz). On a busy server the 4 ms allowance takes over and
+  it can take 2 to 3 s. Blocks appear in layers from the bottom up, in the save's order.
+- **When several players join together, builds load one after another**, in join order. With 8
+  full builds the last one starts after about 7 s.
+- **A departed player's build disappears over about a second** instead of all at once.
+- **For that second the departed player's cells are already free.** Another player can place a
+  block where an old block is still on screen, and it briefly overlaps that block until the old
+  one is destroyed.
+- **No more server-wide freeze or rubber-banding on join or leave**, and clients should no longer
+  hitch in `ProcessPackets` on a join.
+
+### Changes for other code
+
+- **Blocks are no longer all direct children of the plot folder.** Loaded blocks sit one level
+  down, in `SavedBlocks` folders (about 50 for a full build). Blocks placed during play are still
+  direct children. Code that reads a plot's blocks should use `GetDescendants()`, or filter
+  `GetChildren()` for folders.
+- **`Owner` and `PlacedAt` children are gone.** Read `block:GetAttribute("OwnerId")` (a number,
+  not a string) and `block:GetAttribute("PlacedAt")`. Nothing in the repo read the old children.
+- **Removal now requires the block to be in the player's current plot**, not just any plot with
+  their UserId on it. That only differs when a player rejoins before their old plot has finished
+  tearing down. Without it they could remove an old block and free a cell another plot owns.
+- **A player who leaves while their save is loading is handled.** The load finds that the player
+  has left, and stops. `SavedBuilds.load` doesn't yield today, but the DataStore version will.
+
+### Left alone
+
+- **Reserving cells on join is still one pass over the save**, about 20,000 table writes in a
+  single frame. That is plain Luau with no instances, an estimated few milliseconds. If a capture
+  shows it, it can be spread across frames too. Saved cells would then need to win any race with
+  placements in other ways.
+- **`SavedBuilds.load` builds 20,000 tables.** It is a stand-in for the DataStore and will be
+  replaced.
+- **Streaming is off.** Turning on `StreamingEnabled` would cut what each client downloads for
+  plots far away. It changes behavior for client code (parts may not be there yet), so it is a
+  separate decision.
+- **The server still creates every block as a `Part`.** Having the server hold block data and
+  each client build its own visuals would remove most of the replication cost. It is a much
+  bigger change (raycasts, removal targeting, and collision would all move to that design), so it
+  is worth considering only if the numbers below are still over budget.
+
+### Tuning
+
+- `BLOCKS_PER_FRAME` sets the load speed and the per-frame replication burst. Lower it if clients
+  on phones still hitch when a build loads, and raise it if loading feels slow and the server has
+  room.
+- `FRAME_ALLOWANCE` caps server script time per frame. The script timer does not see the engine
+  work that parenting triggers later in the frame, so judge it by whole frame times in a capture.
+
+### For you to verify
+
+Nothing could be run in Studio from here. Luau type checking (`luau-lsp`), `selene`, and `stylua`
+pass.
+
+1. **Server capture on a join with the 20,000-block build.** No frame should be over about 20 ms,
+   and `Builds:Step` should hold the build work. Compare with the 2,100 ms baseline.
+2. **Server capture on that player leaving.** Same check.
+3. **Client capture during a join**, on the weakest phone you support. `ProcessPackets` should no
+   longer spike. If it does, lower `BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`.
+4. **Eight players joining together**: the server stays smooth, and every build finishes.
+5. **By hand**: place and remove blocks while a build is still loading. Check that removing
+   another player's block fails, and that a block cannot be placed in a cell the loading build has
+   not reached yet. Leave mid-load and rejoin straight away, and check that the old build clears
+   and the new one loads in full.
